{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-103704",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-9$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-9)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{7}{6},$ then $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi )=\\frac{7}{6}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi )+2 & =18(\\frac{7}{6})+2 \\\\  & =21+2 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
